{{also|sister in law}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{inh+|en|enm|suster-in-lawe}}; equivalent to {{suf|en|sister|in-law}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sĭs'tər|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪs.tɚ.ɪn.lɔ/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪs.tə.ɪn.lɔː/|a=RP}}
* {{hyphenation|en|sis|ter|in|law}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-PerfectSoundWhatever-sister-in-law.wav|a=CA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sisters-in-law|sister-in-laws|pl2qual=colloquial, nonstandard}}

# A female relative of one's generation, separated by one degree of marriage:
## The [[sister]] of one's [[spouse]].
##: {{ux|en|Though they are not twins, my '''sister-in-law''' resembles my wife in almost every way.}}
## The [[wife]] of one's [[sibling]].
##: {{ux|en|My '''sister-in-law''' and my brother both met while they were on vacation in Jamaica.}}
# {{lb|en|less common}} [[co-sister-in-law|Co-sister-in-law]]: The wife of one's [[sibling-in-law]].
## The [[wife]] of the [[sibling]] of one's [[spouse]].
## The [[sister]] of the [[spouse]] of one's [[sibling]].

====Usage notes====
The plural ''sister-in-laws'' is occasionally seen, especially in American English, but this is considered incorrect by most sources; see, for example, the Web Site of Professor Paul Brians: [https://brians.wsu.edu/?s=sister-in-laws].

